<title>USA: U.S. Oct consumer prices rose 0.3 pct.</title>
<headline>U.S. Oct consumer prices rose 0.3 pct.</headline>
<dateline>WASHINGTON 1996-11-14</dateline>
<text>
<p>U.S. Labor Department monthly Consumer Price Index (CPI-U), 1982-84 equals 100:</p>
<p>    Percent Changes:  Seasonally Adj   Unadjusted</p>
<p>    -			   Oct   Sept   Oct96/95</p>
<p> All Items		    0.3    0.3	3.0</p>
<p> -Excluding Food/Energy   0.2    0.3	2.6</p>
<p> Energy			 0.7   Unch	5.7</p>
<p> Food, Drink		  0.6    0.5	4.0</p>
<p> Food			   0.6    0.5	4.0</p>
<p> CPI-Urban Consumers-X  158.3  157.8	N/A</p>
<p>X-Data unadjusted. N/A-Not Available.  </p>
<p>    Percent Changes:  Seasonally Adj   Unadjusted</p>
<p>    -			   Oct   Sept   Oct96/95</p>
<p> Services		     0.3    0.2	3.2</p>
<p> Housing			0.3    0.2	2.9</p>
<p> -Home Fuel Oil	     6.3    3.6     18.4</p>
<p> -Homeowner Costs-Y	 0.3    0.2	2.9</p>
<p> Apparel, Upkeep	    0.5    0.5     -0.8</p>
<p> Transportation	     0.1    0.5	3.2</p>
<p> -Gasoline		    0.7   -0.4	7.3</p>
<p> -New Cars		   -0.2    0.5	2.1</p>
<p>Y-Dec 1982=100 base.  </p>
<p>    Percent Changes:  Seasonally Adj   Unadjusted</p>
<p>    -			   Oct   Sept   Oct96/95</p>
<p> Medical Care		 0.2    0.2	3.2</p>
<p> Commodities		  0.4    0.4	2.8</p>
<p> CPI-W			  0.3    0.3	3.0</p>
<p>CPI-W is the Consumer Price Index for urban wage earners and clerical workers.  </p>
<p>Wall Street economists expected a 0.3 percent rise in October consumer prices. Excluding food and energy, they expected a 0.2 percent increase.</p>
<p>The department said fuel oil costs rose 7.0 percent in October, the largest rise since an 8.8 percent gain in January 1996.</p>
<p>It said airline fares increased 1.9 percent in October.</p>
